        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> > The buffer is not listed, while previously it was listed\n> \n> is this actually wanted? i think it was a mistake in the old impl. after all, the buffer is wiped (in vim's impl too) after it is closed\n\nI can observe at least two side effects of making that buffer not listed:\n1. It will not be shown in most \"bufferline\" style tablines, as they usually show only listed buffers.\n2. Some automatic actions can be conditioned on whether a buffer is listed, as usually \"listed buffer\" is a good indicator that the buffer is \"important enough\".\n\nBoth of these are not super critical, but add (what I can perceive as) avoidable friction. There doesn't seem to be *significant* benefits of making the cmdwin buffer not listed, while this being a breaking change would probably warrant at least some. With this choice I'd usually go for backward compatibility.",
